{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '195,375p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '540,605p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '805,865p'; tail -35 docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '195,375p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '540,605p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '805,865p'; tail -35 docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md\"","aggregated_output":"   195\t\n   196\t`ctx` is the expander's process state and is threaded through every call:\n   197\t\n   198\t```\n   199\t{:store    {sym lambda-ast}       ; macro environment, persisted across batches\n   200\t :alloc    {:next-eid n}          ; monotonic negative counter, never re-seeded upward\n   201\t :guards   {:max-depth 100  :max-nodes-per-expansion 10000\n   202\t            :max-steps 100000  :max-datoms-per-batch nil}\n   203\t :eval     (fn [lambda-ast env prelude budget] → value)   ; §3.3\n   204\t :t        0}                     ; batch counter; incremented once per expand-batch\n   205\t```\n   206\t\n   207\t`expand-batch`:\n   208\t\n   209\t1. **Admit.** The batch is indexed with `index-datoms` and checked as a\n   210\t   whole — **every entity in the index, not only those reachable from the\n   211\t   root**, because harvest (step 2) reads disconnected definitions: every\n   212\t   `:yin/type` is in the closed vocabulary (§3.3), every ref resolves to an\n   213\t   entity in the index, the whole graph is acyclic, every ground value is\n   214\t   plain data, and every `:macro? true` lambda is the value operand of a\n   215\t   `(yin/def <literal sym> …)` — a macro lambda anywhere else has no meaning\n   216\t   on either side of the boundary and, forwarded, would load on the walker\n   217\t   as a plain closure, the quiet case decision 5 exists to prevent — all\n   218\t   *before* any recursive decoding (Appendix B). An in-memory medium can\n   219\t   carry host values, and the sandbox's closed-input guarantee holds only if\n   220\t   nothing else is admitted. A failure here is\n   221\t   `{:status :error :error {:kind :malformed-input :reason :unknown-type |\n   222\t   :dangling-ref | :cyclic | :host-value | :stray-macro-lambda :eid e}}`\n   223\t   carrying a **batch-level\n   224\t   event** (§4.1) — allocated from the watermark alone, since the index may\n   225\t   be unusable — and the advanced `:ctx`. Diagnostics name entities, never\n   226\t   carry the offending values.\n   227\t2. **Harvest.** `definitions` finds every `(yin/def <literal sym> v)` in the\n   228\t   batch, in batch order, last wins: `v` a `:macro? true` lambda →\n   229\t   `assoc` into `:store`; `v` anything else (a plain lambda, a variable, a\n   230\t   literal) → `dissoc`, so a redefinition as a non-macro stops expansion of\n   231\t   that name. Harvest is *syntactic* and whole-batch by policy: a definition\n   232\t   in an unexecuted branch, or after its first use in the same batch, is\n   233\t   still in force for the whole batch. (Clojure defines a macro only when the\n   234\t   `defmacro` form executes; this design does not, and says so.)\n   235\t3. **Replace.** Each macro definition node is replaced by\n   236\t   `{:type :literal :value <sym>}`, so a batch whose root is a definition\n   237\t   still forwards a complete program and a REPL prints the name. Plain\n   238\t   `yin/def`s are forwarded untouched.\n   239\t4. **Expand.** `expand-node` (§3.2) runs from the root over the index.\n   240\t5. **Post-harvest.** Expansion can *generate* definitions that only take\n   241\t   the `yin/def` shape after it — `(defn m …)` above all. The store for the\n   242\t   **next** batch is recomputed by one last-wins pass over the union of (a)\n   243\t   the source definitions step 2 saw, at their batch positions, and (b) every\n   244\t   `(yin/def <literal sym> v)` in the final tree that was emitted from a\n   245\t   macro's output — identified by `m` = an expansion event on its log copy,\n   246\t   never by eid, since re-parented copies of source nodes also have fresh\n   247\t   eids — at the batch position of the call site it came from. A macro\n   248\t   lambda `assoc`es, anything else `dissoc`es; the last definition of a\n   249\t   name in that order wins. **Ordering rule:** step 2's source-only store\n   250\t   governs the whole current batch; step 5's result governs from the next\n   251\t   batch. Consequences, stated: `(def m f)` then `(defmacro m …)` keeps the\n   252\t   macro (the later source definition wins, and the surviving plain\n   253\t   `yin/def` in the tree is a *source* node step 2 already ranked, not a\n   254\t   generated one); `(defmacro m …)` then `(defn m …)` removes it (the\n   255\t   generated plain definition is later); within one batch a macro `m` and a\n   256\t   `(defn m …)` both apply to their own forms in source order, as Clojure\n   257\t   would not, and the design accepts that one-batch divergence in exchange\n   258\t   for a one-pass, deterministic rule.\n   259\t6. **Mark tail.** `mark-tail` recomputes `:tail?` over the whole final tree\n   260\t   (§3.4).\n   261\t7. **Emit.** Output is emitted bottom-up as a self-contained batch with a\n   262\t   fresh `:yin/root` fact; the log copy carries `m` per §4.1.\n   263\t8. **Scan.** A final walk asserts no lambda with `:yin/macro? true` remains.\n   264\t   With admission rejecting stray macro lambdas, step 3 replacing source\n   265\t   definitions, and `:generated-macro` rejecting generated ones, this cannot\n   266\t   fire; it is the guard for decision 5 and a violation is a forwarder\n   267\t   defect, thrown as `{:kind :scan-failed :eid e}`.\n   268\t\n   269\tAn `:error` result carries the event (§4.1) in `:log` and the advanced\n   270\t`:ctx`; `:datoms` is absent. `expand` unwraps `:ok` and throws on `:error`\n   271\tfor callers that want the exception. Same inputs, same output, on every\n   272\thost.\n   273\t\n   274\t**Working representation.** Traversal, cycle detection, and shadow tracking\n   275\trun over *eids in the index*. Plain AST maps are materialised only at the\n   276\tboundary of one body invocation — operands decoded for `invoke`, the returned\n   277\tvalue validated and re-encoded to datoms with fresh ids, and re-expansion\n   278\tcontinues over the index of the batch-so-far. Internal identity never\n   279\tappears in the map representation macros see (`valid-ast?` rejects `:eid`).\n   280\t\n   281\t**Allocation.** `:alloc` is seeded per batch to\n   282\t`(min (:next-eid alloc) (dec min-eid-in-batch) (- (inc datom/first-user-id)))`\n   283\t— the incoming watermark is a term, so the counter only ever decreases across\n   284\ta session and event eids on the accumulated log never collide. It is returned\n   285\tadvanced in both `:ok` and `:error` results; a retry of a staged flush reuses\n   286\tthe staged allocation and never allocates again.\n   287\t\n   288\t### 3.2 The algorithm\n   289\t\n   290\tTraversal visits ref attributes in a **fixed order** so fresh ids and\n   291\tgensyms are deterministic across hosts: `:yin/operator`, `:yin/operands`\n   292\t(left to right), `:yin/test`, `:yin/consequent`, `:yin/alternate`,\n   293\t`:yin/body`, `:yin/target`, `:yin/val-node`, `:yin/source`. `shadow` is the\n   294\tset of names bound by enclosing `:lambda :params`.\n   295\t\n   296\t```\n   297\tmacro-of ctx eid shadow :\n   298\t  case operator(eid)\n   299\t    :variable v  → v ∉ shadow ∧ (:store ctx) v\n   300\t    :lambda  l   → l when (:macro? l)\n   301\t    _            → nil\n   302\t\n   303\texpand-node ctx eid shadow depth :\n   304\t  if type(eid) = :application ∧ (m = macro-of ctx eid shadow):\n   305\t      ev        = fresh-event ctx eid m                  ; allocated first: every failure below is recorded on ev\n   306\t      if depth ≥ :max-depth: fail ev {:kind :depth-guard :depth :chain}\n   307\t      operands  = [decode c | c ∈ operands(eid)]         ; unexpanded AST maps\n   308\t      out       = invoke m operands ctx                  ; §3.3 — arity, fuel, suspension fail on ev\n   309\t      check valid-ast? out {:allow-macro-lambda? false}  ; node count ≤ :max-nodes-per-expansion\n   310\t      root'     = encode out (fresh ids from :alloc)     ; joins the index of the batch-so-far\n   311\t      record ev :yin/expansion-root root'\n   312\t      return expand-node ctx root' shadow (depth+1)      ; re-expand the output under the call site's scope\n   313\t  else if type(eid) = :application:\n   314\t      op'       = expand-node ctx operator(eid) shadow depth        ; operator FIRST\n   315\t      eid'      = if op' = operator(eid) then eid else copy eid with operator op'\n   316\t      if macro-of ctx eid' shadow:\n   317\t          return expand-node ctx eid' shadow depth        ; became a macro call: takes the first arm with the ORIGINAL operands\n   318\t      operands' = [expand-node ctx c shadow depth | c ∈ operands(eid)]\n   319\t      return if operands' = operands(eid) then eid' else copy eid' with operands'\n   320\t  else:\n   321\t      shadow'   = if type(eid) = :lambda then shadow ∪ params(eid) else shadow\n   322\t      children' = [expand-node ctx c shadow' depth | c ∈ children(eid) in fixed order]\n   323\t      return if children' = children(eid) then eid else copy eid with children'\n   324\t```\n   325\t\n   326\t`fail ev …` writes `:yin/error` on the event and unwinds to `expand-batch`,\n   327\twhich returns the `:error` result with that event in `:log`. Cycle and\n   328\tdangling-ref detection happened at admission (§3.1 step 1), so this walk is\n   329\tover a known-acyclic graph; output encoded from validated plain maps is\n   330\tacyclic by construction.\n   331\t\n   332\tThe application branch expands the **operator before the operands** so that\n   333\tan operator which rewrites into a macro name hands that macro its operands\n   334\t*as written*: in `((choose) f [x] (x 1))` with `choose` → `defn`, `defn`\n   335\tmust receive `(x 1)` unexpanded so the lambda it builds can shadow `x`.\n   336\tExpanding operands first would violate outermost-first for exactly the\n   337\tmacros (binders) that depend on it. Recognition is not an expansion — the\n   338\tre-entry is at the same depth — and a successful re-check goes straight into\n   339\tthe invocation arm, which re-expands at `depth+1`, so a chain of generated\n   340\tapplications consumes depth and cannot rewrite forever at constant depth.\n   341\t\n   342\tProperties, each a Phase 1 test:\n   343\t\n   344\t- **Outermost first.** A macro sees its operands as written. This is what\n   345\t  makes `->`-style macros expressible and what retires the shadow hints:\n   346\t  in `(defn f [x] (x 1))`, `defn` expands *before* its operands are examined,\n   347\t  yielding `(yin/def f (lambda [x] (x 1)))`; re-expansion then meets `(x 1)`\n   348\t  under `:lambda [x]`, so `x` is shadowed and left alone even if a macro\n   349\t  named `x` exists. yang's frontend hint existed only because the frontend\n   350\t  had to decide this before expansion.\n   351\t- **Fixpoint by construction.** Every node reachable from the final root\n   352\t  was visited after its last rewrite, *including* an application whose\n   353\t  operator was rewritten into a macro name by a child expansion\n   354\t  (`((choose) x)` where `choose` expands to `m`): the application branch\n   355\t  re-checks after expanding the operator and before touching the operands.\n   356\t- **Depth** counts nesting of expansion inside expansion output. Direct\n   357\t  recursion without a base case fails at 100 with\n   358\t  `{:kind :depth-guard :depth 100 :chain [eid ...]}`. Siblings do not\n   359\t  accumulate depth.\n   360\t- **Shadowing is lexical and complete.** `let`, `do`, and every binder every\n   361\t  frontend emits lower to `:lambda :params`; `yin/def` is global and is the\n   362\t  store. There is no other binder in the Universal AST. The shadow set is\n   363\t  extended *at the lambda node*, so an operator-position lambda's params\n   364\t  shadow its body and not its sibling operands.\n   365\t- **Generated macro lambdas are rejected in v1 — wherever they appear.** A\n   366\t  macro whose output contains a `:macro? true` lambda, as a definition value\n   367\t  *or* inline in operator position, fails `{:kind :invalid-output :reason\n   368\t  :generated-macro}`; `valid-ast?` is called with `:allow-macro-lambda?\n   369\t  false` on expansion output. `macro-of`'s inline-lambda case therefore only\n   370\t  ever fires on lambdas the *source* batch contained. Macro-defining macros\n   371\t  and generated inline macros are reserved together (§7); admitting the\n   372\t  second without the first would need a rule for keeping a generated macro\n   373\t  body as transformer syntax through re-expansion, which is the same\n   374\t  problem.\n   375\t- **Failure leaves a record.** The attempt event is allocated before the\n   540\t\n   541\t## 4. Provenance\n   542\t\n   543\t### 4.1 The expansion event\n   544\t\n   545\tEvery attempt, successful or not, produces one event; so does every batch\n   546\trejected at admission. Events and the `m`-tagged output are appended to the\n   547\t**log** medium, when the expander has one; `program-out` receives only\n   548\tprogram.\n   549\t\n   550\t```\n   551\t[ev :yin/type           :macro-expand-event  t default-op]\n   552\t[ev :yin/source-batch   <ctx :t>             t default-op]   ; which source batch; always present\n   553\t[ev :yin/source-call    <call-eid>           t default-op]   ; eid in that batch, or in the log copy of the enclosing expansion; absent on an admission failure\n   554\t[ev :yin/macro-name     <sym>                t default-op]   ; when the operator was a :variable; absent for an inline macro lambda or an admission failure\n   555\t[ev :yin/macro          <lambda-eid>         t default-op]   ; only when durable (§4.2)\n   556\t[ev :yin/expansion-root <root'>              t default-op]   ; on success; names the LOG copy\n   557\t[ev :yin/error          {:kind ...}          t default-op]   ; on failure; plain data naming entities, never carrying input values\n   558\t[ev :yin/timestamp      <ctx :t>             t default-op]   ; logical, never a host clock\n   559\t```\n   560\t\n   561\tAn **admission-failure event** is the same entity with only `:yin/type`,\n   562\t`:yin/source-batch`, `:yin/error`, and `:yin/timestamp`: the batch had no\n   563\tusable call or macro to name. It is allocated from `:alloc`'s watermark\n   564\t(which needs no index), so a corrupt batch still produces exactly one\n   565\tplain-data event, advances `:ctx` once, and is retried through the same\n   566\tper-medium staging as any other log payload — never regenerated.\n   567\t\n   568\tThe log copy of expansion output carries `m = ev`; log copies of unchanged\n   569\toperands and ancestors carry `m = default-op` (re-parenting, not macro\n   570\toutput). **`program-out` datoms all carry `default-op`**: `m = ev` would be\n   571\ta cross-medium ref (§4.2). A nested expansion's `:yin/source-call` names a\n   572\tnode in the log copy of the enclosing expansion's output, which is how the\n   573\tchain source → event → root → event → root is walked for generated syntax.\n   574\t\"Every datom this expansion produced\" is `[?d _ _ _ ?ev]` on the log.\n   575\t\n   576\t`yin.vm.macro/event-schema` declares these attributes (`:yin/source-call`,\n   577\t`:yin/macro`, `:yin/expansion-root` as refs) for compositions that commit a\n   578\tlog to `dao.space`; the transactor relocates only declared refs, so a\n   579\tcomposition merges this fragment with `yin.vm/schema` before committing.\n   580\t\n   581\t### 4.2 Identity across media\n   582\t\n   583\t`program-in`, `program-out`, and the log are different media. A ref written\n   584\ton one that names an entity on another is meaningful only when the named\n   585\tid is **durable** — a permanent positive id from a committed stream. The\n   586\ttransactor (`dao.space.transact`) resolves tempids within one transaction;\n   587\tcommitting two batches separately does not connect them. Therefore:\n   588\t\n   589\t- `program-out` batches are self-contained: unchanged subtrees are copied,\n   590\t  with fresh ids, not referenced (decision 8).\n   591\t- `:yin/source-call` on an event names the call's eid *as it appeared in\n   592\t  the source batch*, qualified by `:yin/source-batch`; it is descriptive\n   593\t  until both media are committed under one resolution. `:yin/macro` is\n   594\t  written only when the macro's id is durable; otherwise `:yin/macro-name`\n   595\t  carries the name. Names are metadata, never identity repair.\n   596\t- `:yin/expansion-root` names the **log copy** of the output. The executed\n   597\t  program on `program-out` is a distinct copy with distinct ids; this design\n   598\t  promises no id-level correspondence between them. A composition that needs\n   599\t  one commits program and log in one transaction (they are one batch to the\n   600\t  transactor) or records the mapping itself.\n   601\t- A pipeline that wants queryable provenance commits `program-in` first and\n   602\t  expands against durable ids — the ordering rule of\n   603\t  `cross-language-macro.md` §4, retained. `ADR 0003` makes `dao.space` the\n   604\t  medium for that commit.\n   605\t\n   805\tnative lowering; definition consumed and replaced by a name literal; a\n   806\tdefinition in an unexecuted branch is harvested; last definition wins;\n   807\t**redefinition: `(defmacro m)` then source `(def m f)` removes the macro\n   808\tin-batch; `(def m f)` then `(defmacro m)` keeps it next batch; `(defmacro\n   809\tm)` then `(defn m …)` removes it next batch; an existing macro and a\n   810\tseparate `(defn m …)` batch removes it next batch; each repeated with the\n   811\tdefinition node re-parented (fresh eid) by an unrelated expansion in the\n   812\tsame batch**; a bare `:macro? true` lambda outside a definition fails\n   813\tadmission as `:stray-macro-lambda`;\n   814\toutermost-first (`->`-style macro); shadowing by `let`, `fn`, and by a\n   815\t`defn`'s own params without any frontend hint; `(fn [m] (m 1))` with `m` a\n   816\tmacro is left intact; a macro name in operand position is left as a\n   817\tvariable; **an operator macro returning a macro name is expanded with its\n   818\toperands unexpanded (`((choose) f [x] (x 1))` with a macro `x` in scope\n   819\tyields a lambda whose body still says `(x 1)`), and an operator-selected\n   820\tmacro that discards an operand containing a failing macro call succeeds**;\n   821\ta macro emitting a macro lambda — as a definition or inline — fails\n   822\t`:generated-macro`; depth guard with chain; per-expansion bound from node\n   823\tcount before emission; per-batch bound; step budget on an infinite body;\n   824\tparked body rejected; **cyclic operand, dangling ref, unknown node type, a\n   825\thost value in a literal, and a cycle inside a disconnected definition all\n   826\tfail at admission before any decode, each producing one admission event\n   827\tthat survives a log `full`**; invalid output including a host function inside a\n   828\tliteral and an `:eid` key; arity and `&`; **gensyms differ across two\n   829\tbatches with overlapping tempids when ctx is threaded, and are equal on\n   830\treplay of the same ctx**; a stream effect in a body fails with the\n   831\tconstructor error; `mark-tail` on `if`/`let`/lambda spines **and clears a\n   832\tstale flag on marked syntax moved into an operand and into an `if` test**;\n   833\ta macro-free deeply nested batch passes; determinism (two runs, equal\n   834\tvectors); macro-free batch returned equal modulo the root fact; a\n   835\tcorpus-scale batch at 80% of a configured per-batch bound passes; the\n   836\tforwarder driven through `observer/run-on-stream`: `out=ok, log=full`\n   837\tretries only the log; `out=full` retries only the program; repeated `full`;\n   838\tno log medium; **an expansion failure advances the cursor, stages only the\n   839\tevent, and appears in `drain-errors`; a failed batch then a successful one\n   840\tin a single `run-on-stream` call yields `:forwarded 1` and one error; a\n   841\tdrained expander reports nothing on the next drain**; a forwarder-defect\n   842\tthrow does not advance the cursor.\n   843\t\n   844\t### Phase 2 — REPL composition\n   845\t\n   846\tDeliverables per §6.1. Tests: define in one input, use in the next; use\n   847\tfrom `:python` after defining in `:clojure`; `(reset)` forgets; `(compile)`\n   848\trenders four stages; a runtime error from a macro-named variable in operand\n   849\tposition is an unbound-variable error, not a call; a bad macro input prints\n   850\tits error and the *next* input evaluates normally (no head-of-line block);\n   851\t`repl-state` lists the store's macro names.\n   852\t\n   853\tDeferred to the semantic VM's own Phases 1–2 (`yin.vm.semantic` and\n   854\t`linearize` do not exist yet): the `:semantic` VM type runs the same session\n   855\twith equal values, and a tail-position macro in a 10⁵-iteration loop keeps\n   856\tcontinuation depth 0 — measured by inspecting `k`, since equal values alone\n   857\tdo not prove it.\n   858\t\n   859\t### Phase 3 — Cross-language verification\n   860\t\n   861\tTests (`python_macro_test.clj`, `php_macro_test.clj`): a scalar-signature\n   862\tClojure macro `(defmacro twice [f] (yin/application f [(yin/application f\n   863\t[(yin/literal 1)])]))` called as `twice(inc_fn)` from Python and\n   864\t`twice($inc_fn)` from PHP; `yin/name-of` with a Python string, a Clojure\n   865\tsymbol, and a `:variable`; a PHP `for`-loop variable shadowing a macro name;\n| 8 | Final scan must be scope-aware (astra P2-6, glm P3-8) | §3.1 step 8 scans only for `:macro?` lambdas; application fixpoint is by construction via #4 |\n| 9 | Tail marking must clear stale flags; lambda rule ambiguous (astra P2-7) | §3.4 whole-tree recompute with per-node context rules; lambda body always tail; `linearize` owns inlining |\n| 10 | Provenance identity: same tempid in two batches, generated syntax, program↔log correspondence, event schema home, anonymous macros (astra P2-8) | §4.1 `:yin/source-batch`; nested `source-call` into the log copy; `expansion-root` names the log copy, correspondence not promised; `macro/event-schema`; `:yin/macro-name` absent for inline lambdas |\n| 11 | Output validation alone does not close the sandbox input (astra P2-9) | §3.1 step 1 admission validates ground values; §3.3 validates operands going in; `:eval`/prelude trust contract stated; fuel and payload limits recorded as known v1 limits |\n| 12 | Cycle detection after recursive decode overflows; `eid(node)` undefined on maps (astra P2-10, glm P3-8) | §3.1 admission over the index; working-representation paragraph; Appendix B |\n| 13 | \"As in Clojure\" is wrong for syntactic harvest; \"helpers are other macros\" unsupported; macro/runtime namespace relation unstated (astra P3-11) | §3.1 policy stated on its own terms; §3.3 helpers are prelude only, body expansion reserved; §2.2 two namespaces |\n| 14 | `program-out` `m = ev` would be a cross-medium ref; event attrs dropped from schema but still emitted (glm P3-5) | Decision 9 and §4.1: `program-out` carries `default-op`; `macro/event-schema` |\n| 15 | Root fact unconditional vs \"compile output unchanged\" (glm P3-6) | Phase 0 test reworded \"modulo the root fact\" |\n| 16 | Phase 2 `:semantic` test has an unstated prerequisite (glm P3-7) | Phase 2 defers it to the semantic spec's Phases 1–2, with the continuation-depth measurement |\n| 17 | Missed-macro diagnostics (glm P3-9) | §6.1 `repl-state` lists the store's names |\n| — | Qualify \"never holds VM control state\" (astra, layering) | §1.2 table: the *program* evaluator's state |\n| — | Later batch ≠ expansion inside a loaded continuation (astra P3-11) | Appendix A #6 already states the limit; decision 11 makes the consumed-batch semantics explicit |\n\nRound 3 (`collab/1789279450960-review-macro-stream-process-r3.{gpt-6-astra,glm-5.3}.findings.md`), on revision 3:\n\n| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| 18 | Re-check happens after operands expanded; a newly discovered binder macro gets rewritten operands (astra P1-1) | §3.2 application branch: operator first, re-check, then operands; test with `((choose) f [x] (x 1))` |\n| 19 | `(defn m …)` after `(defmacro m …)` not caught — the `yin/def` shape appears only post-expansion; non-lambda redefinitions ignored (astra P2-2) | §3.1 step 2 `dissoc`es on any non-macro value; new step 5 post-harvest over the final tree, effective next batch; ordering rule stated |\n| 20 | Inline generated macro lambda both required by a test and forbidden by the validator (astra P2-3) | Prohibition kept and widened to `:generated-macro`; the test case removed; both reserved together |\n| 21 | `:errors` never drained; a stale error suppresses later evaluation; A-ok/B-error in one call skips A (astra P2-4, glm P2-1) | §5 `drain-errors` read-and-reset with `:forwarded`; §6.1 drives the evaluator on `:forwarded > 0` independently of `:errors` |\n| 22 | Admission checks only root-reachable, harvest decodes the whole batch (astra P2-5) | §3.1 step 1 checks every entity in the index |\n| 23 | Admission failures have no event path (astra P2-6) | §4.1 admission-failure event allocated from the watermark, no input values in diagnostics |\n| 24 | `run-on-stream` error shape: a value-shaped error lets un-updated callers continue silently (glm P3-2); load vs flush failure must carry different cursors (astra) | §5 prerequisite: keep the throw, carry `:session` in `ex-data`, cursor position depends on where the failure was; Phase 0 tests |\n| 25 | Admission should check the closed node vocabulary (glm P3-3) | §3.1 step 1 `:unknown-type` |\n| 26 | `closed`/`invalid-value`/`transport-error` are protocol outcomes, not defects; `full` may be permanent (astra) | §5 table reworded; outcome and both slots preserved in the throw |\n| 27 | Decision 5 \"unbound variable\" too strong given §2.2; decisions 10/11 out of order; §3.4 exactness under the no-inline contract (astra, glm) | Decision 5 qualified; order fixed; §3.4 sentence added |\n\nRound 4 (`collab/1789279972076-review-macro-stream-process-r4.{gpt-6-astra,glm-5.3}.findings.md`), on revision 4; glm approved r4:\n\n| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| 28 | Post-harvest removes a later winning source macro because an earlier plain source `yin/def` survives in the tree (astra P2) | §3.1 step 5 is a unified last-wins over source definitions at their positions and *generated* definitions (identified by `m` = event, never eid) at their call sites' positions; consequences enumerated; four acceptance cases plus re-parented variants in Phase 1 |\n| 29 | Stray source macro lambda outside a definition shape has no disposition (glm P3-1) | §3.1 step 1 `:stray-macro-lambda` at admission; step 8 named `:scan-failed` as a forwarder defect |\n| 30 | Appendix C row 5 names the retired `:generated-definition` (glm P3-2) | Row 5 annotated |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
